    <help><![CDATA[
.. image:: $PATH_TO_IMAGES/flipbook_logo.png
   :alt: RMSX Flipbook logo
   :width: 280px
   :align: center

.. class:: infomark

**What it does**

RMSX Flipbook runs RMSX on a molecular dynamics trajectory, divides the selected
frame window into time slices, and reports residue-level fluctuation for each
slice. It also creates a typed ``rmsx.json`` viewer manifest, which renders the
generated PDB slices with Molstar through Galaxy's Visualize action.

_____

.. class:: infomark

**Inputs**

Use either the bundled 1UBQ example or datasets from the current history. The
current wrapper supports PDB topology/structure input with DCD or XTC trajectory
input. All valid protein chains are analyzed by default. To limit the run, choose
selected chains or segments and enter comma-separated selectors such as ``A,B``.

RMSX divides the requested frame window into equal-sized slices and discards
trailing remainder frames. The manifest records the actual first and last frame
and time of each analyzed slice.

Before RMSX runs, a preflight check reports available chain and segment IDs and
catches common load, selector, frame-window, and slice-count problems.

The RMSX options control the frame window, analysis type, summary residue count,
interpolation, and shared color palette. The palette is used by both the static
plots and the interactive viewer.

_____

.. class:: infomark

**Outputs**

The tool produces per-chain collections of RMSX, RMSD, RMSF, and mask metadata
tables; one combined PDB slice collection with RMSX values written into the
B-factor column; per-chain static RMSX heatmap and triple-panel PNG collections;
an execution log; and one combined viewer manifest.

Open the manifest with Galaxy's Visualize action and choose ``RMSX Flipbook``.
The manifest uses schema version ``flipbook-molstar-viewer/v1`` and is emitted
as typed Galaxy ``rmsx.json`` data for the native RMSX Flipbook visualization.
    ]]></help>
    <expand macro="citations"/>
</tool>
